    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of Chartism</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>More than 180 years ago, working men in Britain could not vote, because the vote went only to people who owned property.</p><p>Working men began to meet in groups, in London and in Carmarthen in Wales, to ask for change.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chartism.jpg" alt="Chartism" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Chartism <span class="kid-tale-cr">William Edward Kilburn, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>In 1838 the Chartists set out their aims in the People's Charter, a list of six changes.</p><p>It asked for a vote for every man over twenty-one, a secret ballot, and pay for Members of Parliament.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>In 1839 more than a million people signed a petition, a long list of names, but Parliament refused to listen.</p><p>That year marchers went to a hotel in Newport, Wales, held by soldiers, and more than twenty Chartists were killed.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/staffordshire.jpg" alt="Staffordshire" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Staffordshire <span class="kid-tale-cr">David Rayner, CC BY-SA 2.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>A second petition, with over three million names, was turned down too.</p><p>Strikes spread from the Staffordshire Potteries, and hundreds of strikers were put in prison.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>Their best-loved leader was Feargus O'Connor, a tall man whose voice could reach huge crowds.</p><p>In 1847 he was elected for Nottingham, the only Chartist ever to become a Member of Parliament.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eureka-rebellion.jpg" alt="Eureka Rebellion" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Eureka Rebellion <span class="kid-tale-cr">John Black Henderson (1827-1918), Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Some Chartist leaders were sent to Australia as punishment, and miners there later asked for Chartist rights.</p><p>The movement faded, but later five of its six demands became law, among them the secret ballot.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The People’s Charter of 1838 asked for six reforms: a vote for every man over twenty-one, the secret ballot, no property qualification for MPs, payment of MPs, equal constituencies and annual elections.</li><li>Petitions signed by 1.3 million people in 1839 and over three million in 1842 were rejected by the House of Commons, and the risings and strikes that followed were put down with arrests and transportation.</li><li>Chartism directly won no reform, but its demands were enacted later by other reformers: the secret ballot in 1872, payment of MPs in 1911 and full manhood suffrage in 1918.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 2011 a small pamphlet of 16 hymns, previously unknown and uncatalogued, was discovered in Todmorden Library; it is believed to be the only Chartist hymnal in existence.</p></aside>
    </section>

